{
  "query": "You are the database lead for BlueCotton Travel where your booking platform runs in three regions on GKE and uses a multi-region Cloud Spanner instance for consistency and resilience. You need a connection approach that remains highly available and scales with demand while adding as little connection overhead as possible. Which approach should you implement?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Place a global external Application Load Balancer in front of a proxy tier that forwards requests to Cloud Spanner",
      "explanation": "This adds a managed load balancer and a self-operated proxy layer between the application and the database service",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Have each application instance connect directly to the nearest Cloud Spanner regional endpoint",
      "explanation": "This connects clients to the closest Cloud Spanner regional endpoint and uses the client library for efficient channel and session reuse",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Expose a single Private Service Connect endpoint in one region for the Spanner API and route all client traffic through it",
      "explanation": "This publishes one regional Private Service Connect endpoint and funnels cross-region database traffic to that single point",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Force all database connections to use a single shared static egress IP address",
      "explanation": "This centralizes outbound traffic with NAT so all connections originate from one IP instead of optimizing for proximity",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Have each application instance connect directly to the nearest Cloud Spanner regional endpoint</b>.</p><p>This approach keeps traffic as local as possible which reduces latency and minimizes connection overhead because there is no extra hop. The Cloud Spanner client libraries manage connection pooling, load balancing, and retries which lets the service scale horizontally as more application instances come online. In a multi‑region deployment the service maintains high availability and handles failover automatically so direct connections from each instance provide both resilience and performance without introducing new bottlenecks.</p><p>Direct connections also align with Google Cloud networking patterns. Using the closest regional API endpoint helps avoid cross‑region hairpins and makes better use of Spanner replication. Each instance establishes its own channels so throughput increases with demand and there is no intermediary tier to scale or troubleshoot.</p><p><i>Place a global external Application Load Balancer in front of a proxy tier that forwards requests to Cloud Spanner</i> is unnecessary and counterproductive because it adds an extra network and processing hop. A custom proxy tier becomes another component to scale and operate and it can interfere with long‑lived gRPC sessions and transactional behavior. It does not improve Spanner availability and it increases latency and failure surface.</p><p><i>Expose a single Private Service Connect endpoint in one region for the Spanner API and route all client traffic through it</i> centralizes traffic through one region which creates a latency penalty for other regions and introduces a regional point of failure. If private access is required the better pattern is to place endpoints in each region that serves clients rather than a single endpoint.</p><p><i>Force all database connections to use a single shared static egress IP address</i> does not add availability or scalability. It can create a bottleneck due to connection tracking limits and possible port exhaustion and it introduces an additional point of failure. Cloud Spanner does not require fixed client egress IPs for correctness so this constraint only adds risk.</p>",
